#!/usr/bin/env python
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
r"""
DR1 - the department analyst's two screens say what they are for.

THREE RULINGS (2026-08-14), all about a screen showing the workflow rather than
the work.

1. "WE MAY CONSIDER TO RENAME TO DEPARTMENT REVIEW to avoid the confusion with
   the Credit Analysis." Two things called Analysis - the module and a tab
   inside it - is one word doing two jobs. The tab is now Department Review.

2. "THIS SHOULD NOT HAVE COME ON THE ACTIONS BUT ON THE ANALYSIS." Picking a
   case sat on the Actions tab, which otherwise said "no actions available for
   your role at its current status" - so an analyst opening an unpicked case
   read a dead end on one tab while the button to claim it was on another.

   Pick now sits at the top of Department Review, where the work starts.

3. "THIS SHOULD BE MY CASES AND THE POOL. It is the pool that will contain all
   the cases submitted from the branch, and from here is where they select and
   it comes to My cases - once selected it moves out of the pool. This is for a
   department especially with 2 analysts to all pick from the pool."

   The "All" tab was a third view of the same cases answering no question an
   analyst asks. Gone.

   So were thirteen status buttons - approved, declined, offer_signed,
   analyst_confirmed. That is the shape of the workflow, not something somebody
   opens this page to ask, and the full picture is a click away in Sales Pro.

   The Pool's caption said "read-only - request assignment from a case to work
   it", which described a flow that no longer applies: a department analyst
   picks, they do not request. It now says what the pool IS and what happens
   when you pick from it.

statusFilter itself stays and remains 'all': it is the mechanism the search box
and the tabs narrow through, and rewriting a working filter to delete a row of
buttons would be the larger change.

Verified: tsc --noEmit clean, vite build clean.

Usage (from project root, .venv active):
    python scripts\\patch_dr1_department_review.py            # dry run
    python scripts\\patch_dr1_department_review.py --apply
"""
import json
import os
import shutil
import sys

BACKUP_SUFFIX = ".pre_dr1"


def _p(rel):
    """A stored forward-slash key as a path this OS can open. os.path.join
    gives backslashes on Windows and a straight lookup fails with a KeyError -
    which is how the previous patch never ran on the machine it was for."""
    return os.path.join(*rel.split("/"))


